#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093075} :activity/id #uuid "e1c96bbd-1fde-5a80-973f-5b7f6ca284d0" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048775 :topic/id #uuid "bd96558d-c5ce-53df-9675-85805e56651b" :topic/math-academy-id 991} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093075 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093440 :tutorial/content "Consider the matrix ![](images/30/30880937eed54b00cb0ebe13bd2a46e2634e4193343b1db552b30836ed1e055b.svg) Given that $\\lambda=3$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ how can we find an eigenvector that corresponds to this eigenvalue?\n\nRecall that a non-zero vector $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ with corresponding eigenvalue $\\lambda$ if \n\n$$\nA{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}}={\\color{red}\\lambda}{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}}.\n$$\n\nWe can rewrite this equation, substituting ${\\color{red}{\\lambda = 3}},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =3v \\\\ Av-3v & =0 \\\\ Av-3Iv & =0 \\\\ (A-3I)v & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to $\\lambda = {\\color{red}3},$ we need to find a non-zero solution of the equation \n\n$$\n(A- {\\color{red}3} I){\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}} = \\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $(A- {\\color{red}3} I){\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}} = \\mathbf{0}$ corresponds to the following homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 0=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $A-{\\color{red}3}I$ is already in row echelon form, and it has one pivot column (the $1$st one). So, $x_2$ is a free variable. Moreover, from the first equation, we get $x_1=6x_2,$ which implies that the general solution is\n\n![](images/fa/fa0625bc99de3a27874b5695e72c9fd2e41ab618e49228086df7efcf7e1bb04f.svg)\n\nWe only require one eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda={\\color{red}3}.$ Setting $x_2=1,$ we obtain the eigenvector\n\n![](images/bc/bcd87529387e7c400b7f5ccd896cfcbe3e42ab386a5b9d84b565f12752e2c4c3.svg) \n\n**Note:** If we set $x_2=2,$ we'll get a different eigenvector, namely\n![](images/83/83541fd42d19f0e3b17be1c36983d50d1382f36352168bce2d148126db7bf265.svg)\nNote that $\\mathbf w$ is also an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda=3.$ In fact, we can choose any real $x_2 \\neq 0$ to generate an eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda = 3.$ \n\n**Watch out!** In the case of $x_2=0,$ we get the zero vector, but this is not an eigenvector. Remember that eigenvectors must be non-zero vectors." :tutorial/id #uuid "c585e113-f22f-5b5f-bfef-b3b81cd21acc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3562 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a47c9656-c51a-58d2-a0dd-f88dacd21b14" :step/math-academy-id 20088 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093077}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093076 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092233 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092078 :question/id #uuid "3ea5361d-2508-5d54-acbf-46a57be601f5" :question/math-academy-id "e-5483"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d491b223-3526-5db0-93d1-6b08c1f7b7b5" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092609} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092610} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244031} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244033} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348360} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348361} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386243} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392010} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393996}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Eigenvector Corresponding to an Eigenvalue"} :step/id #uuid "96b57363-6414-5170-aa47-40a20b85159c" :step/math-academy-id 20089 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093078}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093077 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092234 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092079 :question/id #uuid "f23dcdbe-03e6-582f-8383-b07a1b2036c4" :question/math-academy-id "e-5484"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc3c9f32-7ac7-5c2c-b337-70fcfbc802b9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048181} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048819}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092687} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092904} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244020} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244114} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287675} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357565} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357567} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426811}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Components of an Eigenvector Corresponding to an Eigenvalue"} :step/id #uuid "4346b6e9-b1f3-5e72-a0bc-acca6bf07e34" :step/math-academy-id 20090 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093076}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093078 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093441 :tutorial/content "Given a matrix $A$ with an eigenvalue $\\lambda$, the **eigenspace** $V_\\lambda$ can be defined as the set of all possible eigenvectors corresponding to $\\lambda$ plus the vector $\\mathbf{0}{:}$\n![](images/78/78c2c84d57ef00f32675c6d56fa91af39e0680eadf05ec266b0bdab9a57a8388.svg)\n\nNote that the eigenspace consists of *all* (not only non-zero) solutions of the equation $(A-\\lambda I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.$ So, the eigenspace can also be viewed as the *null space* of the matrix $A-\\lambda I,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nV_{\\color{red}\\lambda} = \\textrm{Null}(A - {\\color{red}\\lambda} I).\n$$\n\nWe also have the following theorem:\n\n*Eigenvectors that correspond to different eigenvalues are always linearly independent.*\n\nFor example, the matrix \n\n![](images/e3/e3c63b7efcb927079757e6f0e3e008a6f5038e11957524f51e1b1f45fdecd5f7.svg)\n\nhas eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=-2$ and $\\lambda_2=-1,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are \n\n![](images/1c/1c5df8239dae6efdd15a27498a9535b855004e3570c8eff46534993b9ec1e75f.svg) \n\nHere, $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ are linearly independent (they are not collinear).\n\n**Watch out!** Eigenvectors that correspond to the *same* eigenvalue *can be* (and very often *will* be) linearly dependent. For example, the vectors\n\n![](images/c3/c303fb73d6fc039bfa536d6f7de3103e774d913d2514c10c17d59fcf48d5bf00.svg)\n\nare collinear eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ above that correspond to $\\lambda_2=-1.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "98b63ece-dfc2-522f-bf9d-260df06b75a7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3563 :tutorial/title "Connection Between Eigenspaces and Null Spaces"} :step/id #uuid "b551a89c-7046-5a16-b776-1bf76d285e27" :step/math-academy-id 20091 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093079}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093079 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092235 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092080 :question/id #uuid "b59a8ad2-7233-54c2-9055-5e188e0b8503" :question/math-academy-id "e-5485"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "022c4cb4-d6f6-56a5-a6dc-3adfc5aabda7" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092905} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092906} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244043} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244044} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244113} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348362} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348363} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357576} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357584} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426812} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426813}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Linearly Independent Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "80c21f2c-61fd-5d44-98f9-6a51e9812c73" :step/math-academy-id 20092}] :activity/title "Calculating the Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186092465}]}
